1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,310
That line shows up in almost every AI feature PRD, usually in the testing section,

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,488
usually agreed to without objection.

3
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:10,270
Who's actually going to build it, and by when?

4
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:11,601
It sounds like a plan.

5
00:00:11,601 --> 00:00:14,323
It's actually a gap wearing a plan's clothes.

6
00:00:14,323 --> 00:00:19,343
Nobody's named, so it belongs to everyone, which means it belongs to no one until a

7
00:00:19,343 --> 00:00:21,279
launch date forces the question.

8
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:26,299
Nobody's said how big or what it has to cover, so whoever ends up building it under

9
00:00:26,299 --> 00:00:31,380
pressure grabs whatever's fastest: ten cases from Slack, the ones already sitting in

10
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,167
a bug report.

11
00:00:32,167 --> 00:00:35,312
The dataset doesn't need to be written into the PRD.

12
00:00:35,312 --> 00:00:36,885
The commitment to it does.

13
00:00:36,885 --> 00:00:39,546
Watch how this plays out on a real schedule.

14
00:00:39,546 --> 00:00:44,446
The line sails through review because it sounds reasonable and nobody wants to be

15
00:00:44,446 --> 00:00:47,470
the person who blocks a PRD over a testing detail.

16
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:52,491
Then, in the third week before launch, someone finally asks who's actually building

17
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:57,511
it, and the honest answer is nobody has started, because nobody was ever the answer

18
00:00:57,511 --> 00:00:58,539
to that question.

19
00:00:58,539 --> 00:01:03,378
What should have been two weeks of unhurried work becomes two days of panic, and

20
00:01:03,378 --> 00:01:07,794
panic is exactly the condition under which a golden set gets built badly.

21
00:01:08,344 --> 00:01:13,170
Same intention, and only one version survives contact with a slipping schedule.

22
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:17,813
The second version has four things the first one doesn't: a floor on size, a

23
00:01:17,813 --> 00:01:22,884
coverage requirement that rules out fifty happy-path cases counting as done, a name

24
00:01:22,884 --> 00:01:27,038
attached to the work, and a date that sits before launch, not on it.

25
00:01:27,038 --> 00:01:29,176
None of that is the dataset itself.

26
00:01:29,176 --> 00:01:33,086
It's the commitment that makes the dataset someone's actual job.

27
00:01:33,086 --> 00:01:36,141
Notice, too, that the good version is still short.

28
00:01:36,141 --> 00:01:39,440
It's one sentence longer than the bad one, not a page.

29
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:44,083
The goal here isn't length, it's specificity: every noun in that sentence is

30
00:01:44,083 --> 00:01:47,137
something a project tracker could hold someone to.

31
00:01:47,137 --> 00:01:51,047
"Appropriate" was never a word you could put a deadline against.

32
00:01:51,047 --> 00:01:52,391
A name and a date are.

33
00:01:52,941 --> 00:01:57,808
Five questions, and notice none of them ask you to actually build anything yet.

34
00:01:57,808 --> 00:02:02,921
They ask you to decide, in writing, what building it correctly is going to require.

35
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:06,062
The coverage question is the one worth dwelling on.

36
00:02:06,062 --> 00:02:11,113
Fifty cases that are all ordinary, everyday inputs will pass beautifully and prove

37
00:02:11,113 --> 00:02:16,165
almost nothing, because they never touch the failure modes that actually hurt you.

38
00:02:16,165 --> 00:02:21,154
The PRD doesn't need to enumerate every bucket, that's real, deliberate work, and

39
00:02:21,154 --> 00:02:24,665
Section four walks through exactly how to do it properly.

40
00:02:24,665 --> 00:02:29,593
What the PRD needs is the requirement that coverage exists at all, so nobody can

41
00:02:29,593 --> 00:02:31,503
call fifty easy cases finished.

42
00:02:31,503 --> 00:02:36,431
The source question matters almost as much, and it's the one people skip fastest

43
00:02:36,431 --> 00:02:37,663
under time pressure.

44
00:02:37,663 --> 00:02:42,530
Real transcripts show you what actually happens: the messy phrasing, the second

45
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:45,117
language, the question nobody anticipated.

46
00:02:45,117 --> 00:02:50,106
Invented examples show you what you already imagined, which tells you nothing you

47
00:02:50,106 --> 00:02:51,523
didn't already believe.

48
00:02:51,523 --> 00:02:56,266
If real data genuinely doesn't exist yet, because the feature hasn't launched

49
00:02:56,266 --> 00:03:01,071
anywhere, say that plainly in the PRD rather than quietly filling the gap with

50
00:03:01,071 --> 00:03:02,796
guesses dressed up as cases.

51
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:08,370
Say the honest caveat, because the opposite mistake is just as common as the vague

52
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:12,229
one: dumping fifty rows straight into the PRD to look thorough.

53
00:03:12,229 --> 00:03:15,048
A PRD is read once, argued over, and archived.

54
00:03:15,048 --> 00:03:20,071
A golden set gets reused every release, edited under version control, argued about

55
00:03:20,071 --> 00:03:20,868
case by case.

56
00:03:20,868 --> 00:03:25,585
Cram it into the document and you get the worst of both: a bloated PRD nobody

57
00:03:25,585 --> 00:03:29,200
rereads, and a dataset that's awkward to actually maintain.

58
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,917
Commit to it here, build and version it properly elsewhere, and link the two.

59
00:03:33,917 --> 00:03:38,083
Think of the PRD as the contract and the dataset as the deliverable.

60
00:03:38,083 --> 00:03:41,146
Nobody writes the deliverable inside the contract.

61
00:03:41,146 --> 00:03:45,986
They write what the deliverable has to satisfy, and where to go find it once it

62
00:03:45,986 --> 00:03:46,415
exists.

63
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:51,378
That distinction is what keeps this section from turning into fifty rows of stale

64
00:03:51,378 --> 00:03:54,135
examples nobody trusts by the second release.

65
00:03:54,685 --> 00:03:59,701
That's the whole shape of it: a paragraph, not an appendix, and every sentence in it

66
00:03:59,701 --> 00:04:01,732
answerable by someone with a name.

67
00:04:01,732 --> 00:04:03,822
The exercise is the rewrite itself.

68
00:04:03,822 --> 00:04:08,540
Take the vague line if your PRD has one, and run it through the five questions.

69
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:13,497
Where you don't have an answer yet, write the question down instead of skipping it.

70
00:04:13,497 --> 00:04:16,006
An unanswered question in a PRD is honest.

71
00:04:16,006 --> 00:04:18,813
A vague sentence pretending to answer it isn't.

72
00:04:19,363 --> 00:04:21,143
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:21,143 --> 00:04:22,187
Rewrite the line.

74
00:04:22,187 --> 00:04:27,098
Give it an owner, a number, and a date, even if the date moves later, that alone

75
00:04:27,098 --> 00:04:30,045
closes most of the gap this lecture opened with.

76
00:04:30,045 --> 00:04:35,079
Then be honest about coverage: if you already suspect you have no real examples of

77
00:04:35,079 --> 00:04:38,026
this feature failing badly, write that down now.

78
00:04:38,026 --> 00:04:42,324
It's a better finding today than a silent gap in the golden set later.

79
00:04:42,324 --> 00:04:47,235
Next lecture goes underneath the dataset to the other half of the threshold: the

80
00:04:47,235 --> 00:04:51,655
rubric that makes "good" mean the same thing no matter who's scoring it.
